  "enhanced_information": "The 4-year-old boy presents with periorbital and lower extremity edema, significant proteinuria (4+), and low serum albumin (2.6 g/dL), consistent with nephrotic syndrome. The normal serum complement levels and the presence of fatty casts suggest minimal change disease, which is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in this age group. Prednisone is the first-line treatment for minimal change disease. Other options such as enalapril and furosemide are not first-line treatments for this condition, and anti-streptolysin O levels are not indicated as the recent sore throat does not suggest a post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis. Cyclosporine is typically reserved for steroid-resistant cases. Therefore, the most appropriate next step in management is prednisone therapy.",
  "analysis": "The clinical presentation of the 4-year-old boy, including periorbital and lower extremity edema, significant proteinuria (4+), and low serum albumin (2.6 g/dL), is indicative of nephrotic syndrome. The presence of fatty casts in the urine and normal serum complement levels further support a diagnosis of minimal change disease, which is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children. The first-line treatment for minimal change disease is prednisone therapy, as it is effective in inducing remission. Other options such as enalapril and furosemide are not first-line treatments for this condition, and anti-streptolysin O levels are not relevant given the recent sore throat does not suggest post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis. Cyclosporine is typically reserved for cases that are resistant to steroids. Therefore, the most appropriate next step in management is to initiate prednisone therapy.",
